A shape whose body is `{}` renders nothing, so the draw function below is
empty and the canvas around it is not.

`{}` and no block at all are different declarations, and this is the first
half of that pair. An empty body says the component renders nothing, which is
a legitimate thing to say; a bodyless one says the render comes from
somewhere the declaration names, and with no override for the target being
built that is a build failure -- `error_component_no_body_shape.sngl` is
that half.

This file used to assert the opposite: that `blip` survived lowering because
`isPrimitiveComponent` exempted a segmented tree's members from
passInlinePure, reading the `ctx.save()`/`ctx.restore()` pair it produced as
the shape having reached passCanvas. That pair was the bracket `emitShape`
puts around a composed shape, wrapped around a body with nothing in it -- so
the exemption kept a declaration that renders nothing from being dropped, and
the page carried a bracket nobody asked for. The header said the component
had "no body by design", which the `{}` on the declaration below denies.

deny out/none/html/index.html `ctx\.save\(\)` -- a component that renders nothing gets no bracket; the canvas is what survives here, not the shape

-- main.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import "sngl:ui/draw"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
}
component blip(x float, y float) draw.shape {}

window(title="t") {
    draw.canvas(width=100px, height=100px) {
        blip(x=1.0, y=2.0)
    }
}
